{"abstract":[{"text":"Whether a grant permits an action or refuses it (B1, deny-override). # Precedence Default","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"deny -> an [","type":"text"},{"code":"PermissionEffect::Allow","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"] grant permits -> a [","type":"text"},{"code":"PermissionEffect::Deny","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"] grant","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"refuses, ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"and beats every allow"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":", wherever either sits in the resource hierarchy. Deny"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"wins; there is no most-specific-wins tie-break. That choice is deliberate and is argued in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"full in "},{"code":"claude_dev\/deny-override-design.md","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" §2.1. The short version: deny-override buys one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"checkable property — "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"adding a deny rule can never widen access, and can never be undone by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"adding allows","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — and most-specific-wins buys expressiveness at the cost of making “is X"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"denied?” unanswerable without enumerating every other rule that might out-specify it."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"["},{"code":"PermissionEffect::Allow","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"] is the default, so data written before this existed, and clients"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"that send no "},{"code":"effect","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", both mean “allow”. No migration."}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"topicSections":[{"title":"Enumeration Cases","identifiers":["doc:\/\/AxiamSDK\/documentation\/AxiamSDK\/PermissionEffect\/allow",